      "writtenTasks": [
        {
          "number": "1",
          "sourceRefs": [
            "p0112#4"
          ],
          "sourceText": "1. 什么叫做波？",
          "frozenText": "1. What is a wave?",
          "figureRefs": []
        },
        {
          "number": "2",
          "sourceRefs": [
            "p0112#5"
          ],
          "sourceText": "2. 横波与纵波的区别是什么？举出横波与纵波的例子。",
          "frozenText": "2. How do transverse and longitudinal waves differ? Give examples of each.",
          "figureRefs": []
        },
        {
          "number": "3",
          "sourceRefs": [
            "p0112#6"
          ],
          "sourceText": "3. 波动的基本特点是什么？",
          "frozenText": "3. What is the basic characteristic of wave motion?",
          "figureRefs": []
        },
        {
          "number": "4",
          "sourceRefs": [
            "p0112#7"
          ],
          "sourceText": "4. 什么叫做波长？",
          "frozenText": "4. What is wavelength?",
          "figureRefs": []
        },
        {
          "number": "5",
          "sourceRefs": [
            "p0112#8"
          ],
          "sourceText": "5. 波速与波长有何联系？",
          "frozenText": "5. How are wave speed and wavelength related?",
          "figureRefs": []
        },
        {
          "number": "6",
          "sourceRefs": [
            "p0112#9"
          ],
          "sourceText": "6. 指出图70上两个相邻的球振动的相位差。",
          "frozenText": "6. State the phase difference between the vibrations of two adjacent balls in Figure 70.",
          "figureRefs": [
            "fig-70"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "blockIndex": 0,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "As the transverse wave travels, once the vibration has reached the thirteenth ball, the first and thirteenth balls undergo identical vibrations."
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "blockIndex": 0,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "When the first ball is at its equilibrium position and moving left (see Figure 70e), the thirteenth ball is also at equilibrium and moving left."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "blockIndex": 0,
          "paragraphInBlock": 2,
          "text": "After another $\\frac{1}{4}$ of a period, the first ball reaches its maximum displacement to the left, and the thirteenth ball reaches the same position (see Figure 70f)."
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "blockIndex": 0,
          "paragraphInBlock": 3,
          "text": "The two balls always vibrate in the same phase.\n**The distance between the nearest points vibrating in the same phase is called the wavelength**."
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "blockIndex": 0,
          "paragraphInBlock": 4,
          "text": "Thus the distances from the first ball to the thirteenth, from the second to the fourteenth, and from the third to the fifteenth all equal one wavelength (see Figures 70e and 70f)."
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "blockIndex": 1,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "For the longitudinal wave in Figure 71b, the wavelength equals the distance between the second and fourteenth balls, or between the fourth and sixteenth balls."
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "blockIndex": 1,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "Wavelength is denoted by the Greek letter $\\lambda$."
        },
        {
          "index": 7,
          "blockIndex": 2,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "As a wave travels, different points in the medium, such as the balls in this model, vibrate in different phases unless their separation equals $n\\lambda$ ($n$ is an integer)."
        },
        {
          "index": 8,
          "blockIndex": 2,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "The first and seventh balls, separated by $\\lambda/2$ (see Figure 70), vibrate in opposite phases: when the first ball moves left, the seventh moves right, and vice versa."
        },
        {
          "index": 9,
          "blockIndex": 3,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "In one period, a wave travels a distance $\\lambda$ (see Figure 70e).\nIts speed is therefore given by\n$v=\\frac{\\lambda}{T}$ (4.1)"
        },
        {
          "index": 10,
          "blockIndex": 3,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "Since the period $T$ and frequency $\\nu$ are related by\n$T=\\frac{1}{\\nu}$,\nit follows that"
        },
        {
          "index": 11,
          "blockIndex": 3,
          "paragraphInBlock": 2,
          "text": "$v=\\lambda\\nu$ (4.2)"
        },
        {
          "index": 12,
          "blockIndex": 4,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "**Wave speed equals wavelength multiplied by the vibration frequency**."
        },
        {
          "index": 13,
          "blockIndex": 5,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "When a wave travels along a string, we can observe two kinds of periodicity."
        },
        {
          "index": 14,
          "blockIndex": 6,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "First, each point on the string vibrates periodically in time."
        },
        {
          "index": 15,
          "blockIndex": 6,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "For harmonic vibrations, which follow a sine or cosine law, all the points have the same frequency and amplitude and differ only in phase."
        },
        {
          "index": 16,
          "blockIndex": 7,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "Second, at any instant, the shape of the wave repeats along the string at intervals of $\\lambda$.\nThe curve through $O$ in Figure 72 shows the wave's shape at one instant."
        },
        {
          "index": 17,
          "blockIndex": 7,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "As time passes, the entire shape moves from left to right at speed $v$."
        },
        {
          "index": 18,
          "blockIndex": 10,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "After a time interval\n$\\Delta t=\\frac{\\Delta s}{v}$\nthe wave has the shape shown by the curve that does not pass through $O$."
        },
        {
          "index": 19,
          "blockIndex": 11,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "Equation (4.2), which relates wave speed, wavelength, and vibration frequency, also applies to longitudinal waves."
        }
      ],
